    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          GNC AMP Wheybolic is a solid, high-dose whey with a marketing problem: its 40 g of protein per serving comes from two scoops, so it is not stronger than other whey, just bigger. Per GNC's labels at the time of writing, a two-scoop serving gives 40 g of protein in roughly 200 to 220 calories depending on the version. Judge it on cost per 25 g of protein, not grams per serving, and buy it when GNC's sale price beats Optimum Nutrition or Dymatize.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC AMP Wheybolic is a competent whey protein powder. It is easy to find in GNC stores, widely discounted in the chain's frequent sales, and it delivers a big 40 g of protein per serving with low fat and sugar, per GNC's labels at the time of writing. Nothing on the label we could confirm is a red flag, and buyers generally rate the flavors as easy to drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the serving size. That 40 g headline is a two-scoop serving, roughly double what most whey labels list for one scoop. Gram for gram, AMP Wheybolic is about as protein-dense as any other good whey, not more. A tub with 25 servings of 40 g holds the same protein as a tub with 40 servings of 25 g, so the only honest way to compare it is the cost per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            On that measure, it depends entirely on the price you pay. At GNC's regular shelf price, AMP Wheybolic is usually more expensive per gram than Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 on Amazon. During GNC's frequent promotions, it can be competitive. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder rankings</a> list the brands that are cheaper per gram most of the time.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the verdict is simple. Buy AMP Wheybolic if you shop at GNC anyway, you catch it on sale, and you like a big shake. Use one scoop instead of two if you do not need 40 g at once. Buy a mainstream whey on Amazon if you want the best price per gram every day, or a certified isolate if you compete in drug-tested sport.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good whey with a doubled serving. Compare it per 25 g, not per scoop.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">AMP Wheybolic is worth buying on a GNC sale. At full price, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard or Dymatize ISO100 usually costs less per gram of protein.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="the-brand" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes GNC Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            GNC, originally General Nutrition Centers, is one of the oldest supplement retailers in the US. It grew from a single health food store opened in Pittsburgh in 1935 into a national chain of stores, and it has sold its own house-brand supplements for decades alongside third-party brands like Optimum Nutrition and Dymatize. After filing for bankruptcy in 2020, the company was bought by Harbin Pharmaceutical Group, a Chinese pharmaceutical company that was already a major investor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its protein powders sit in several house lines. AMP, short for Advanced Muscle Performance, is the performance line, and AMP Wheybolic is its flagship whey. GNC Pro Performance is the long-running mainstream sports line, which includes a basic 100% Whey. GNC also sells weight-management and specialty proteins under other names, and the line-up changes as products are reformulated and renamed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because GNC owns both the brand and the stores, its protein is priced and promoted differently from brands sold everywhere. Shelf prices are often high, but the chain runs frequent sales, member pricing and bundle deals, so the price you actually pay can vary widely week to week. That matters more for GNC protein than for almost any other brand when you work out value.
          </p>

          <p>
            We have not tasted or tested GNC's proteins ourselves. This review is built from GNC's product labels as published on its listings, retailer listings and nutrition-tracker entries that copy the label. Taste and mixing notes are buyer feedback. GNC's site blocked automated access when we checked, so where we could not confirm a figure, such as the current Pro Performance whey label, we say so instead of guessing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What the AMP Wheybolic Label Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            AMP Wheybolic is sold as a whey protein powder with a serving of two scoops. GNC's labels for the Natural Chocolate and Natural Vanilla versions list 40 g of protein and about 220 calories per two-scoop serving, with about 2 g of fat and 25 servings per container, at the time of writing. Nutrition trackers that copy the regular-flavor label show about 20 g of protein and 100 calories per single scoop of roughly 31 g, with about 3 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
