        <div id="forty-grams" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Need 40 g of Protein in One Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most people, no. Research on protein timing generally finds that around 20 to 25 g of high-quality protein, or about 0.25 to 0.4 g per kg of body weight, captures most of the muscle-building response to a single meal for many people. A 40 g shake is not wasted, because the extra protein still counts toward your daily total, but the extra muscle benefit from the second 20 g is usually modest.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are exceptions. A study by Macnaughton and colleagues in 2016 found that 40 g of whey after a whole-body resistance workout produced a larger muscle-building response than 20 g in trained young men. Larger lifters, people training many muscles in one session, and older adults, who tend to need more protein per meal to get the same response, may all reasonably use a bigger serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            What matters far more is your total daily protein. Many guidelines for people who train suggest roughly 1.6 g per kg of body weight a day, spread over three or four meals. If a 40 g shake helps you reach that number easily, it is doing its job. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> and <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">timing guide</a> walk through the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical takeaway is that you control the serving. If 40 g is more than you need, use one scoop and the tub lasts twice as long, which makes the per-serving price look much better. If you are a large lifter trying to hit 180 g or more a day, the two-scoop serving is a convenient way to get there. Either way, the scoop size does not make the protein better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because AMP Wheybolic's serving is twice the usual size, its price per serving always looks high. The fair comparison is cost per 25 g of protein. A tub with 25 servings of 40 g holds about 1,000 g of protein. Divide the price you pay by 40, and you have the cost per 25 g. For example, a hypothetical $60 tub works out at $1.50 per 25 g, and the same tub at $45 on promotion works out at about $1.13.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">By the Numbers (natural flavors)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">40 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 2 scoops</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">25</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1,000 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Price / 40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            We are not quoting a single GNC price because it moves so much. GNC's shelf prices change often, and the chain runs regular sales and member deals that can cut the price sharply. Check the actual price on the day, then run the calculation. Mainstream wheys like Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard usually land at around $1 to $1.25 per 25 g in large tubs on Amazon, which is the benchmark AMP Wheybolic needs to beat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ready-to-drink AMP Wheybolic 40 shake is a different calculation. Bottled protein always costs more per gram than powder, because you are paying for the liquid, the bottle and the convenience. Use it for days when you cannot mix a shake, not as your everyday protein. Our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink guide</a> compares bottled shakes on cost and label.
          </p>

          <p>
            If price per gram is your main priority, our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">cheap protein powder guide</a> ranks powders purely on cost per 25 g. GNC's own proteins rarely top that list at regular prices, but a good sale can put AMP Wheybolic in the same range as the best-value mainstream tubs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wheybolic, Ripped, Shakes and Pro Performance
          </h2>

          <p>
            AMP Wheybolic is the flagship and the one to buy if you are choosing a GNC protein. It comes in regular flavors and in Natural Chocolate and Natural Vanilla versions, which GNC lists at 40 g of protein and about 220 calories per two scoops. Buyers tend to pick it for the high dose and the wide flavor choice in GNC stores.
          </p>

          <p>
            AMP Wheybolic Ripped is the cutting-focused version. GNC's label lists 40 g of protein in about 200 calories per two-scoop serving and about 22 servings per tub, at the time of writing. Versions marketed for cutting often include extra ingredients, so read the label before assuming it is just a leaner whey. For most people, regular Wheybolic or a plain isolate does the same job in a calorie-controlled diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            The AMP Wheybolic 40 ready-to-drink shake comes in 4-packs and 12-packs. GNC's label lists 40 g of protein, about 190 calories, 1.5 g of fat, 6 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar per bottle. That is a strong bottled-shake profile, with more protein than most ready-to-drink options, which typically run 20 to 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC Pro Performance 100% Whey is the long-running budget-leaning line many people search for. We could not confirm its current label from a source we could access, so we have not given it a product card or figures here. If you see it in a store, compare it with AMP Wheybolic on cost per 25 g using the label on the tub. Our <a href="/pure-protein-powder-review/">Pure Protein review</a> and <a href="/muscle-milk-protein-powder-review/">Muscle Milk review</a> cover other mainstream wheys in the same price tier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing and Clinical Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            Retail listings for AMP Wheybolic describe it as third-party tested, and GNC is large enough to run its own quality programs. What we could not confirm is a sport certification, such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, for the current AMP Wheybolic tubs. Those programs test for banned substances and are the standard drug-tested athletes should rely on.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you compete under drug testing, choose a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases, and check the specific flavor and batch. Dymatize ISO100 and many Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard flavors are commonly cited with Informed Choice certification, per those brands, which makes them safer choices for tested athletes than a product without a confirmed certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            GNC also markets AMP Wheybolic as clinically proven to increase muscle strength and support muscle building. We could not review the study behind that claim, so we cannot tell you how it was designed or what it compared. More broadly, research consistently shows that adequate protein, including whey, supports strength and muscle gains when combined with resistance training. That is true of good whey in general, not a unique property of one product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For heavy metals and contaminants, whey generally tests cleaner than plant proteins and cocoa-heavy products, though chocolate flavors of any protein tend to carry more lead than vanilla. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what testing claims mean and what to ask a brand for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip AMP Wheybolic if you mainly want the cheapest protein per gram and do not shop at GNC. At regular shelf prices, mainstream wheys sold on Amazon are usually cheaper per 25 g of protein, and our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">whey protein rankings</a> name the best-value options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you compete in a drug-tested sport and need a certified product, since we could not confirm a sport certification. Choose a product listed with NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport and verify the flavor in the certifier's database. Skip it, or check the label carefully, if you are lactose intolerant, since we could not confirm whether every flavor is isolate-based. A pure isolate is the safer choice for lactose sensitivity.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the 40 g serving if you are a smaller person, eat plenty of protein already, or are watching calories closely. One scoop of about 20 g is enough for many people per shake, and you can halve the serving without any downside. Using two scoops by default can add 100 or more calories a day you do not need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant or breastfeeding, or who takes medication should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before using a high-protein supplement regularly. This review is not medical advice. Healthy adults generally tolerate whey protein well at normal doses, but a 40 g serving is a large single dose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. GNC vs Optimum Nutrition vs Dymatize
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey is the benchmark mainstream whey. Per the label at the time of writing, a scoop delivers about 24 g of protein in roughly 120 calories, from a blend led by whey isolate, and Optimum Nutrition promotes Informed Choice certification on many flavors. In the large 5 lb tubs it is usually one of the cheapest per gram among the major brands. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dymatize ISO100 is the isolate alternative. It lists about 25 g of protein in roughly 120 calories per scoop with about 1 g of sugar, per the label, and Dymatize states it carries Informed Choice certification. It is the better pick for lactose-sensitive buyers and tested athletes, and it usually costs a little more per gram than Gold Standard. Our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> compares ISO100 with Elite Whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with those two, AMP Wheybolic's advantages are the big serving, the GNC store convenience and the sale pricing. Its disadvantages are a regular price that is usually higher per gram and no sport certification we could confirm. Use two scoops of Gold Standard and you have a 48 g shake anyway, so the 40 g serving is not a real point of difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sensible approach is to buy whichever of the three is cheapest per 25 g of protein on the day, unless you need certification, in which case choose ISO100 or a certified Gold Standard flavor. For bigger-picture comparisons, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">muscle gain protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate rankings</a> go further.
          </p>
        </div>

